        reason: "Read in full: Katz, Muraleedharan and Alase (arXiv:2512.06488) price nothing at the generality this record covers — an arbitrary observable space $G$ and basis $\\Psi$, truncated to $N$ basis functions. Their 3.2 and 3.3 establish only that the nonlinear ODE \"can be approximately represented as an $N$-dimensional linear ODE\", with the proofs placed in \"sections 1.4.1.1 and 1.4.1.2 of [31]\" — a reference this record does not cite — rather than derived here; no query count, gate count or error bound accompanies them. Everything the paper does price is scoped to the Fourier instance it calls Carleman-Fourier linearization, basis $\\Psi_j(x) = [e^{ix}]^{\\otimes j}$: its problem statement is already a Fourier ODE, and both complexity theorems are titled for the Fourier algorithm with and without dissipative conditions. Those numbers belong to that record. The conclusion offers trigonometric, Chebyshev and Hermite bases as directions, not results.",
